/-!
## Index of theorems

  Part III (Woo-Lam Original):
    * `WooLam_Original_Revised.attack_trace_reachable`
        the Abadi-Needham attack trace is admissible under the
        protocol rules + Dolev-Yao intruder;
    * `WooLam_Original_Revised.wooLam_authentication_failure`
        headline consequence -- B believes A authenticated N_b,
        yet A never participated.

  Part IV (NS-PK):
    * `NS_PK.lowe_trace_reachable`
        the Lowe attack trace is admissible;
    * `NS_PK.ns_pk_authentication_failure`
        headline consequence -- B believes session with A, yet A
        never directly sent step 1 to B;
    * `NS_PK.intruder_learns_Nb`
        secrecy failure -- the intruder derives N_b.

  Part V (NS-Sym):
    * `NS_Sym.replay_trace_reachable`
        the Denning-Sacco replay trace is admissible;
    * `NS_Sym.ns_sym_replay_attack`
        headline consequence -- B accepts a replayed ticket as fresh;
    * `NS_Sym.intruder_learns_Nb`
        secrecy failure -- the intruder decrypts B's challenge with
        the compromised session key.

  Part VI (NS-Lowe safety contrast):
    * `NS_Lowe.msg2_shape_distinct`
        structural distinctness of the two step-2 message shapes;
    * `NS_Lowe.ns_lowe_blocks_old_attack`
        Lowe's fix blocks the original NS-PK forgery for every Na, Nb;
    * `NS_Lowe.ns_lowe_trace_safety`
        Paulson-style trace-level safety against the specific forgery.

  Part VII (SSL 3.0 POODLE):
    * `SSL3_POODLE.poodle_trace_reachable`
        an oracle-accepting attack trace is admissible;
    * `SSL3_POODLE.poodle_oracle_accepts`
        existence of a reachable trace with an accepted query;
    * `SSL3_POODLE.ssl3_poodle_attack_certified`
        the iterative oracle attack is protocol-level reachable.

## Relationship to Paulson 1998

Each `Reachable` predicate in this file is a Lean 4 transcription of
the inductive trace-set definitions Paulson used in Isabelle/HOL.
The `fake` constructor corresponds to Paulson's `Fake` intruder
rule; the protocol-step constructors correspond to Paulson's
operational rules.  The pattern of stating an attack as a
`reachable_trace` theorem plus a downstream consequence theorem
mirrors Paulson's standard development pattern.

The safety theorems in Part VI use structural distinctness rather
than full induction-on-`Reachable`; a full Paulson-style safety
proof for the NS-Lowe protocol (covering arbitrary intruder
strategies and parallel sessions) is identified as the natural
next milestone.
-/
